          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Which Inverter Generator to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            An inverter generator is the right portable for anyone who has to live next to it. It is quieter than a conventional open-frame generator, it burns less fuel at light load, and its output is clean enough for laptops, TVs, furnace control boards and medical devices. The trade-off is fewer watts per dollar, and most models are 120V only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our pick for most people is the <strong>Honda EU2200i</strong>: rated at 48 to 57 dBA, about 47 lb, with CO-MINDER shutoff and a reliability record nobody else matches. If you need more output for home backup, the <strong>Westinghouse iGen4500</strong> gives 3,700 running watts and up to 18 hours on a tank at quarter load. On a budget, the <strong>Champion 2500-Watt Ultralight</strong> is the value pick and the lightest on this list, and the <strong>WEN 56380i</strong> gets you 3,400 running watts with electric start for much less than the big names.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide sits inside our broader <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide for power outages</a>, which compares inverters with standby units, big open-frame portables and battery stations. If you are still deciding what kind of backup you need, read that first. If you already know you want a quiet portable, the picks are below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
