        <div id="what-is-an-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Makes an Inverter Generator Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            A conventional portable generator spins its engine at a fixed speed, usually 3,600 rpm, because the alternator has to turn at exactly that speed to make 60 Hz power. The engine runs flat out whether you are powering a single lamp or a well pump. That is why open-frame generators are loud and thirsty at light load.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inverter generator makes raw AC at whatever speed the engine is turning, converts it to DC, then uses electronics to rebuild a clean 60 Hz sine wave. Because the frequency no longer depends on engine speed, the engine can slow down when demand drops. Honda calls this Eco Throttle, Yamaha calls it economy control, and Generac calls it Economy mode, but the idea is the same. At light load, the engine idles down, fuel use falls, and noise drops sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            The electronics also produce cleaner power. Total harmonic distortion, a measure of how far the waveform strays from a perfect sine wave, is typically low on inverters; Westinghouse lists under 3 percent for its dual fuel iGen4500DF, for example. That matters for sensitive electronics, variable-speed furnace blowers and some medical equipment, which can misbehave or be damaged on rough power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most inverters also come in enclosed, sound-dampened cases with mufflers designed for quiet. The Generac GP3500iO is an exception, an open-frame inverter that keeps the clean power and throttle-down economy but skips the enclosure to save cost and weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            What inverters give up is raw output per dollar. A 9,000-watt open-frame generator can cost less than a 3,700-watt inverter. If you need 240V for a well pump or want to feed most of a house through a 50-amp inlet, a conventional or dual fuel portable is the better tool; our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generator guide</a> covers those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Buying Guide: Watts, Run Time, Weight and Safety
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running watts first</h3>

          <p>
            List what you want to power at the same time and add up the running watts, then add the largest single starting surge. A modern refrigerator, a modem, a few LED lights and phone chargers usually fit inside 1,800 running watts. Add a chest freezer and a gas furnace blower and you need closer to 3,000. Add a sump pump or a window air conditioner and the 3,500 to 4,500-watt class makes more sense. Do not plan to run an inverter at 100 percent for hours; leave roughly 20 percent headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run time and tank size</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers usually quote run time at quarter load, which flatters every inverter. Look at the half-load or rated-load figure too. The EU2200i runs about 8.1 hours at quarter load but about 3.2 hours at full rated load. If you want to run a refrigerator overnight without refueling, a larger tank like the iGen4500's 3.4 gallons or the EF2200iS's 1.24 gallons with a fuel gauge makes life easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and carrying</h3>

          <p>
            The 2,000-watt class weighs about 40 to 55 lb and can be carried by one adult. Above 3,000 watts, most inverters weigh 75 to 100 lb and roll on wheels. Think about where it lives between outages and where it will run. Steps, gravel and wet grass matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CO shutoff, parallel capability and start type</h3>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide shutoff, sold as CO-MINDER, CO Shield, COsense and similar names, is now available across most brands. Parallel capability lets you link two matched units for more output later. Electric start helps in cold weather and for anyone who struggles with a pull cord. All three are worth weighing alongside the wattage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rated Run Time at 1/4 Load</p>
